---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="06-string-algos"
  lessonSlug="02-palindrome"
  hook="Two lines. Normalize first, then compare to the reverse. [::-1] is the standard reverse-slice idiom."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def is_palindrome(text):
    clean = "".join(c.lower() for c in text if c.isalpha())
    return clean == clean[::-1]

for s in ["racecar", "Racecar", "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama", "hello", "", "Was it a car or a cat I saw?"]:
    print(f"{s!r:>40}: {is_palindrome(s)}")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="The [::-1] reverse idiom">
    <p>
      <code>s[::-1]</code> is a slice with step = -1 — Python iterates
      backwards. <code>"hello"[::-1]</code> → <code>"olleh"</code>. Memorize it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Normalizing — keep only letters</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="The build-from-chars idiom"
    code={`text = "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
clean = "".join(c.lower() for c in text if c.isalpha())
print(clean)
# "amanaplanacanalpanama"
print(clean == clean[::-1])
# True`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Why not + in a loop?">
    <p>
      <code>clean = clean + c</code> creates a new string every iteration —
      O(n²) for long inputs. <code>"".join(generator)</code> is O(n) and is
      the Pythonic idiom for "build a string from a sequence of chars".
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
